<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John of Gaunt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1340–1399</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster, was the third surviving son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippa-of-hainaut">Philippa of Hainaut</a>, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>. The richest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, he dominated government during the minority of his nephew Richard II and claimed the crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> through his second wife. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-england">Henry Bolingbroke</a> became Henry IV, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lancaster">House of Lancaster</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt.jpg" alt="John of Gaunt" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Said variously to be attributed to Lucas Cornelisz de Kock (1495–1552) (who however died 41 years before the painting... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johnofgaunt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
